## Claim
Text: "A third of excess deaths in the United States between 1 March and  1 August 2020 during the COVID-19 pandemic could not be directly attributed to the coronavirus"
Claim author: Michael Lee  Washington Examiner
Claim date: October 18, 2020
Claim origin: https://web.archive.org/web/20210310055253/https://archive.is/EKPAJ

## Initial Q&A
### 1. What was the total number of excess deaths recorded in the United States from March 1, 2020, to August 1, 2020, according to the official data source for this claim?
Answer: According to the JAMA study cited in the search result, which analyzed data from the National Center for Health Statistics and the US Census Bureau, the total number of excess deaths in the United States from March 1 to August 1, 2020, was 225,530.

Source URL: https://www.cnn.com/2020/10/12/health/us-deaths-covid-19-pandemic-jama-study/index.html

### 2. Which specific government or scientific body published the report or data stating that one-third of excess deaths in the U.S. from March to August 2020 were not directly attributed to COVID-19?
Answer: The report was published by researchers from Virginia Commonwealth University and Yale University in the journal JAMA, as indicated by the statement that "Researchers from Virginia Commonwealth University (VCU) and Yale University published findings on Tuesday in JAMA."

Source URL: https://www.foxnews.com/health/coronavirus-us-excess-deaths-study

### 3. According to the source of this claim, how was the baseline number of "expected" deaths for the March to August 2020 period calculated?
Answer: According to the source, the baseline number of "expected" deaths was calculated by comparing the all-cause death counts during the March to May 2020 period to death counts from the same period in previous years, using data from the National Center for Health Statistics and the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention.

Source URL: https://www.cidrap.umn.edu/covid-19/about-30-covid-deaths-may-not-be-classified-such

### 4. In the source data for this claim, how is a death classified as being "directly attributed to the coronavirus"?
Answer: According to the source, deaths are classified as "directly attributed to the coronavirus" based on official CDC figures compiled from death certificates. The article states, "The number of deaths the CDC officially attributed to COVID-19 in the United States was 200,499 through Oct. 3," indicating that these deaths are those where COVID-19 is listed as a cause on the death certificate as processed through the National Vital Statistics System.

Source URL: https://theconversation.com/279-700-extra-deaths-in-the-us-so-far-in-this-pandemic-year-147887

### 5. What was the exact number or percentage of excess deaths attributed to causes other than COVID-19 infection between March 1 and August 1, 2020, in the cited source?
Answer: According to the cited source, "COVID-19 was a documented cause of only 67% of these excess deaths," which implies that 33% of excess deaths in the United States between March and August 2020 were attributed to causes other than COVID-19 infection.

Source URL: https://www.foxnews.com/health/coronavirus-us-excess-deaths-study

### 6. What were the most frequently listed underlying causes of death for the excess deaths that were not directly attributed to the coronavirus in the cited time period?
Answer: The most frequently listed underlying causes for the excess deaths not directly attributed to the coronavirus included disruptions in medical care for chronic diseases such as diabetes, and emotional crises leading to overdoses or suicides. Additionally, the study noted increases in mortality rates for heart disease and for Alzheimer’s disease and dementia during the same period.

Source URL: https://www.cnn.com/2020/10/12/health/us-deaths-covid-19-pandemic-jama-study/index.html

### 7. Did the source for this claim analyze whether disruptions in healthcare access or delayed treatments for other conditions during the pandemic contributed to the excess deaths not linked to COVID-19?
Answer: Yes, the source analyzed this possibility, noting that excess deaths "include...the indirect consequences of the pandemic on society," which "could include fear of going to the hospital for another condition or any number of issues caused or exacerbated by COVID’s economic and mental health impacts."

Source URL: https://penntoday.upenn.edu/news/US-covid-deaths-may-be-underestimated-36-percent

### 8. Is the "one-third" figure for unattributed excess deaths a finding from a single peer-reviewed study, a government surveillance report, or a secondary analysis of death certificate data?
Answer: The "one-third" figure is a finding from a single peer-reviewed study published in the Journal of the American Medical Association (JAMA), which used CDC data and regression models for its analysis.

Source URL: https://societyhealth.blog/in-follow-up-study-center-finds-that-excess-death-trend-continues-through-covid-19-pandemic/

### 9. Have subsequent analyses or different methodological approaches by other researchers arrived at a significantly different estimate for the proportion of excess deaths not directly due to COVID-19 in the same U.S. time frame?
Answer: Yes, a subsequent analysis published in JAMA in October 2020 arrived at a very similar estimate. The study by Woolf et al. found that "Covid-19 was a documented cause of only 67% of these excess deaths," meaning approximately one-third of excess deaths in the U.S. between March 1 and August 1, 2020, were not directly attributed to the coronavirus.

Source URL: https://www.cnn.com/2020/10/12/health/us-deaths-covid-19-pandemic-jama-study/index.html

## Final Judgement
Based on the recorded evidence, the key finding is that a peer-reviewed study published in JAMA, using CDC data, found that of the 225,530 excess deaths in the United States from March 1 to August 1, 2020, only 67% were directly attributed to COVID-19 on death certificates. This means approximately 33% (or one-third) of excess deaths were not directly attributed to the coronavirus, with causes including disruptions in care for chronic diseases and increases in deaths from conditions like heart disease and dementia.

The evidence is clear and consistent, with a subsequent analysis confirming the same 67% figure. No further investigation is required to verify the core statistic.

The claim states that "a third of excess deaths... could not be directly attributed to the coronavirus." The evidence directly supports this, showing that 33% of excess deaths were not classified as COVID-19 deaths. The claim does not misrepresent the statistic or omit critical context that would change its fundamental meaning.

